= Solution
{parent=a}

**A transmissive mask is placed in contact with the resist, or a small distance above it for proximity printing.** Chromium-patterned glass or quartz is a typical mask. Illumination is normally collimated <ultraviolet> light, with refractive illumination optics used to prepare the beam.

There is no projection objective imaging a reduced mask pattern onto the resist: the mask shadows the resist approximately one-to-one. The finite gap in proximity printing introduces <diffraction> and limits edge sharpness; contact printing reduces that gap at the cost of mask wear and contamination risk.

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Use multilayer reflective optics and a reflective patterned mask.** Near the usual <extreme ultraviolet> <wavelength> of 13.5 nm, ordinary lenses and thick transmissive masks absorb too strongly. Multilayer mirrors focus and project the image, while an absorber pattern on a multilayer-coated mask defines the exposed regions.

The optical path operates in <vacuum> to avoid absorption by air. The key distinction is reflective mask projection, rather than a transparent chromium-on-glass mask or a scanned <electron> probe.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Use <electron> lenses and a digitally scanned, blanked <electron> beam; no physical mask is required for direct writing.** Electromagnetic lenses focus the charged-particle beam, deflectors place it, and the pattern generator controls position and dose. Electrostatic elements can also form part of <electron> optics, but these are not refractive glass lenses.

The <extreme ultraviolet> mask mentioned in the problem is the object being manufactured by this maskless write process, rather than a mask placed in the <electron> path.

