= Solution
{parent=a}

Grow the <semiconductor> stack epitaxially by **<molecular beam epitaxy> or metalorganic vapor-phase <epitaxy>** on a suitable crystalline GaAs substrate. Sequential changes of source flux or precursor supply set the GaAs/AlGaAs compositions, while dopant supply sets the n-type and p-type regions. Growth time and calibrated rate control the thicknesses of the InGaAs <quantum well> and the alternating reflector layers.

<epitaxy> preserves a common crystal orientation and clean, abrupt <semiconductor> interfaces. These are important both for carrier confinement in the <quantum well> and for low-loss optical reflection. For a <DBR> centered at free-space <wavelength> $\lambda_0$, the approximate quarter-wave condition is $n_jd_j=\lambda_0/4$ in each layer. Ordinary deposition of a polycrystalline coating would not provide the required active <semiconductor heterostructure>.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The contact dimensions are readily accessible to **UV <photolithography> followed by directional evaporation and lift-off**. A possible sequence is:

* Clean the <semiconductor> contact surface and coat it with a positive-tone lift-off resist stack. A bilayer giving a small undercut is useful. Soft-bake and align the mask to the intended mesa.
* Use a transparent annular opening in an otherwise opaque mask to expose the contact ring, leaving the central optical aperture covered by resist. Develop away the exposed positive resist. Contact or proximity exposure is adequate for the micrometre-scale ring; projection exposure also works.
* Evaporate the <gold> contact layer directionally onto the wafer. Keep the resist appreciably thicker than the metal and preserve the undercut so that metal on top of the resist does not bridge continuously to the desired ring. Evaporation is more convenient for lift-off than a strongly conformal coating.
* Dissolve the remaining resist and lift away its overlying metal. The annulus on the exposed <semiconductor> remains, while the central emitting aperture is free of metal.

A real device must also have appropriate **<ohmic contact> metallurgy**. <gold> alone on arbitrary GaAs is not automatically an <ohmic contact>: surface preparation, heavy contact-layer doping and, where required, a suitable alloyed contact stack are needed. The schematic <gold> label specifies the visible <electrode>, not all such process details. Order the mesa etch and contact steps so that the etchant and any anneal do not damage an already formed contact.

= c
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use a patterned resist or hard mask and **anisotropic chlorine-based <reactive-ion etching>**, for example an appropriate $\mathrm{Cl_2/BCl_3}$ <inductively coupled plasma> process for GaAs/AlGaAs. Ion-assisted removal gives a well-defined mesa with less lateral undercut than an isotropic wet etch. Control etch time or endpoint to obtain the specified approximately $1.2\,\mu\mathrm m$ depth.

The mask must survive the etch and protect the active aperture. Control <ion> damage and sidewall roughness, remove residues and passivate the sidewall where required. Compatibility with the metal stack determines whether the mesa is etched before or after front-contact deposition.

= ii
{parent=9}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With conventional <electric current> defined into the positive terminal, an ideal junction has
$$
I=I_0\left[\exp\!\left(\frac{qV}{n k_BT}\right)-1\right].
$$
The reverse-bias branch tends to **$-I_0$** before breakdown. In <forward bias> the <electric current> rises exponentially; a visibly sharp “turn-on” is a convenient operating description rather than a discontinuity in the equation. A powered <LED> emits in **quadrant I**, where $V>0$, $I>0$ and the device absorbs electrical <power> $VI$. Strong reverse breakdown lies outside the ideal curve below.

\Image[paper-1-diode-iv.png]
{title=Ideal diode characteristic with reverse saturation and forward LED operation}

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<forward bias> opposes the built-in <electric field> and lowers the junction barrier from approximately $V_{\rm bi}$ to $V_{\rm bi}-V$. In the abrupt-junction <depletion approximation>,
$$
W=\sqrt{\frac{2\epsilon_s}{q}\left(\frac1{N_A}+\frac1{N_D}\right)(V_{\rm bi}-V)}.
$$
Thus **the <depletion region> narrows** and carriers cross the reduced barrier more readily. <electron> injection into the p side and hole injection into the n side increase strongly, allowing <radiative recombination> in an <LED>.

The injected minority-carrier <concentrations>, and therefore the ideal <diffusion> <electric current>, vary approximately as $\exp(qV/k_BT)$. This produces the steep forward branch of the <electric current>–<voltage> curve. One should not extrapolate the depletion-width formula to claim that the junction must lose its entire <depletion region> before conducting; appreciable <electric current> flows while a barrier remains, and high injection and series resistance eventually invalidate the simple formulas.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Taking room <temperature> as $300\,\mathrm K$, the <thermal voltage> is $V_T=k_BT/q=0.025852\,\mathrm V$. For the stated <ideal diode>, $n=1$, so
$$
J=10^{-12}\left[\exp\!\left(\frac{0.75}{0.025852}\right)-1\right]
=\boxed{3.98\,\mathrm{A\,cm^{-2}}}.
$$
The $-1$ term is negligible here. A different precise interpretation of room <temperature> changes the result noticeably because of the exponential; the calculation above states its <temperature> explicitly.

For a lower-bandgap material, the <intrinsic carrier concentration> generally rises, increasing the reverse saturation <current density>, approximately through $J_0\propto n_i^2$ with $n_i^2\propto\exp(-E_g/k_BT)$ when other parameters are held comparable. Thus **the forward <electric current> at the same <voltage> would normally increase**. If $J_0$ were artificially held fixed at the supplied numerical value, however, the ideal-diode equation would give the same <electric current>: the <band gap> dependence enters through the material parameters rather than as a separate term in that equation.

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Interpret the supplied <free energies> as standard reaction <free energies> and use $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. The dimensionless <equilibrium constants> are
$$
K_C=\exp\!\left(-\frac{15000}{RT}\right)=2.3477\times10^{-3},
\qquad K_D=\exp\!\left(\frac{35000}{RT}\right)=1.3651\times10^6.
$$
Under the ideal-solution approximation, $[C]=K_C[L]$ and $[D]=(K_D/c^\circ)[L]^2$. Set $k_D=K_D/c^\circ$, with units $\mathrm{M^{-1}}$. Conservation counts two original <molecules> in each <dimer>:
$$
c_0=[L]+[C]+2[D]=(1+K_C)[L]+2k_D[L]^2.
$$
The physical, positive root can be written without subtractive cancellation as
$$
\boxed{[L]=\frac{2c_0}{1+K_C+\sqrt{(1+K_C)^2+8k_Dc_0}}}.
$$
The resulting <concentrations> are:

|| Initial analytical <concentration>
|| $[L]$/M
|| $[C]$/M
|| $[D]$/M

| $0.100\,\mathrm M$
| **$1.9120\times10^{-4}$**
| **$4.4888\times10^{-7}$**
| **$0.0499042$**

| $1.000\,\mathrm M$
| **$6.0502\times10^{-4}$**
| **$1.4204\times10^{-6}$**
| **$0.499697$**

For example, the first row satisfies $[L]+[C]+2[D]=0.100\,\mathrm M$; using $[L]+[C]+[D]$ would incorrectly count <dimers>. At both <concentrations> almost all molecular units belong to <dimers>.

<dimer> formation is favored by sufficiently high <concentration> because it joins two <molecules>. Dilution favors the separated species, whereas $[C]/[L]=K_C$ is concentration-independent in this ideal model. More than half the molecular units are dimerized when $2[D]>[L]+[C]$, equivalently $c_0>(1+K_C)^2/k_D\simeq7.36\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm M$. The supplied <free energies> at one <temperature> do not determine a <temperature> trend without additional <enthalpy> information. At $1\,\mathrm M$, neglected nonideal activities could be significant; the table is the requested concentration-based model.

= ii
{parent=10}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The end groups associate reversibly through a complementary hydrogen-bond array. At low <concentration>, bringing the two ends of the same <molecule> together avoids the concentration-dependent cost of finding another <molecule>, so an **intramolecular ring** is favored.

For A, increasing <concentration> permits end-to-end intermolecular association to compete successfully with ring closure. It produces long, reversible supramolecular chains. Increasing chain length and entanglement account for the highly viscous solution without requiring covalent <polymerization>.

B has more favorably positioned attachment points on its rigid aromatic core. Its arms can close a less strained ring, so intramolecular association remains strongly competitive over the stated range. Predominantly discrete rings do not form the same extended chain network, explaining the retained fluidity. Both compounds can make the same local <hydrogen bonds>; their different balance of ring <strain>, preorganization and intermolecular association explains the macroscopic difference.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The terminal **<ureidopyrimidinone> groups** supply the association sites. Their <urea>/pyrimidinone NH donors and <carbonyl>/ring-nitrogen acceptors can form a self-complementary array of four <hydrogen bonds> between two <UPy units>. Joining the two terminal units of one <molecule> by this noncovalent association closes the ring.

The ether-containing arms provide flexibility and spacing; they are not covalently joined together during ring formation. The ring is therefore a supramolecular <conformation> of the existing <molecule>, rather than a new covalent <ether> <macrocycle>.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<DMSO> is a strong hydrogen-bond acceptor. It competes for the NH donors of the <UPy units> and solvates the association sites, weakening their mutual hydrogen bonding.

For A, **the intermolecular chains should shorten or dissociate**, reducing the association-driven <viscosity>. For B, **the intramolecular ring should become less populated**, increasing the fraction of open <conformations>. Sufficient <DMSO> also weakens rings of A and any intermolecular associations of B. The covalent molecular skeletons remain intact.

The extent depends on <DMSO> fraction and the association <free energies> in the mixed <solvent>. <solvent> <viscosity> itself also changes, so the prediction concerns the supramolecular contribution rather than an unconditional numerical value for total solution <viscosity>.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use **two-dimensional <NOESY>**, or <ROESY> where molecular tumbling makes <NOESY> less favorable, to look for through-space correlations between proton sites on the two ends that are brought together in the closed <conformation>. Assign <resonances> first and compare predicted ring contacts with an open reference or model.

The <nuclear Overhauser effect> involves <NMR cross-relaxation> through magnetic dipole–dipole coupling. Its strong approximate $r^{-6}$ dependence makes it sensitive to short interproton distances. A <NOESY> cross-peak therefore reports spatial proximity, including between sites far apart along the covalent skeleton; it is not a <NMR scalar coupling> transmitted along that skeleton.

Run a dilution series: end-to-end contacts that persist at <concentrations> where intermolecular association is negligible support intramolecular ring closure. Hydrogen-bonded NH <chemical shifts> and <diffusion> measurements can support the assignment, but neither alone proves ring topology. A flexible open chain can transiently bring ends together, and intermolecular contacts can also give NOEs. Several geometry-consistent cross-peaks, <concentration> behavior and controlled mixing times make the case much stronger than one peak.

= e
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The rigid <naphthalene> core fixes the separation and directions of the two arm attachment points. In A they point from more separated regions of the core, so closure requires the flexible arms to span the core and adopt less favorable torsional arrangements. In B the closer, more convergent attachment geometry permits the UPy ends to meet with **less conformational <strain> and better preorganization**.

Both rings gain similar local UPy hydrogen-bonding interactions, but A pays a larger deformation cost in its linkers and overall geometry. That can rationalize its higher calculated total <energy> and its greater tendency to open into intermolecular chains.

The quoted $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ is large compared with $RT\simeq2.48\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Nevertheless a total-energy difference is not automatically a solution Gibbs free-energy difference: <solvent> and <entropy> contributions would be needed before converting it into an equilibrium population ratio.

= 11
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=11}
{scope}

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use a **lithium-metal negative <electrode> and a carbon-monofluoride composite positive <electrode>**, separated by a porous, electrically insulating <battery separator> wetted by a lithium-salt <electrolyte> in a suitable nonaqueous <solvent>. Conducting <carbon> and a binder provide electronic contact through the fluorinated-carbon <electrode> to its <electric current> collector; the <lithium> <electrode> has its own <electric current> collector. A sealed stacked or wound assembly supplies useful area and contains the <electrolyte>.

During discharge <lithium> is the <anode> and the carbon-monofluoride composite is the <cathode>. <lithium> <ions> move through the <electrolyte> while <electrons> flow through the external load. The <battery separator> prevents electronic shorting while permitting ionic transport. An aqueous <electrolyte> is unsuitable because metallic <lithium> reacts with water. The requested very long operating time requires enough active material and low self-discharge, not merely a high nominal <voltage>.

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the specified $x=1$, the discharge <half-reactions> and their sum are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{Anode:}\quad&\mathrm{Li\longrightarrow Li^++e^-},\\
\text{Cathode:}\quad&\mathrm{CF+Li^++e^-\longrightarrow C+LiF},\\
\text{Overall:}\quad&\boxed{\mathrm{Li+CF\longrightarrow C+LiF}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Each <mole> of CF formula units consumes one <mole> of <lithium> and transfers one <mole> of <electrons>. For general $x$, the corresponding formula-unit reaction would be $\mathrm{CF}_x+x\mathrm{Li}\longrightarrow\mathrm C+x\mathrm{LiF}$.

= ii
{parent=11}
{scope}

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Using $365$ days per year, the required time is $2\times365\times24=17520\,\mathrm h$. Hence
$$
\boxed{Q=(0.010)(17520)=175.2\,\mathrm{Ah}=630720\,\mathrm C}.
$$
<lithium> transfers one <electron> per atom, so
$$
n_{\rm Li}=\frac{Q}{F}=\frac{630720}{96500}=6.53596\,\mathrm{mol},
\qquad\boxed{m_{\rm Li}=6.53596(6.94)=45.4\,\mathrm g}.
$$
This is the stoichiometric minimum for one cell, or one cell-equivalent parallel assembly, with complete utilization and no self-discharge. Practical loading must be larger. If a pack uses several cells in series, each series cell must carry the full delivered charge and the total <lithium> mass increases accordingly; no required pack <voltage> or series count is supplied.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The reaction consumes one <mole> of CF for each <mole> of <electrons>. Using $M_{\rm CF}=12.01+19.00=31.01\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$,
$$
\boxed{m_{\rm CF}=\frac{630720}{96500}(31.01)=202.7\,\mathrm g}.
$$
This is active CF alone for the same single-cell-equivalent assumption, not the full positive-electrode or pack mass. Conductive additive, binder, <electrolyte>, <battery separator>, collectors and casing add mass, and incomplete utilization increases the required CF.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the $x=1$ reaction there is one <mole> of transferred <electrons> per <mole> of reaction. **Treating the supplied fresh-cell <open-circuit voltage> as the standard equilibrium cell potential** gives
$$
\boxed{\Delta G^\circ=-FE^\circ=-(96500)(4.57)=-441005\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}
\simeq-441\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}}.
$$
A fresh-cell <voltage> alone would not normally establish standard conditions or equilibrium. The result above is the formal standard-free-energy estimate under the interpretation required to use the given datum; a metastable <open-circuit voltage> should not be mistaken for an independently measured standard thermodynamic potential.

= d
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**This is a primary cell and should not be treated as rechargeable.** Discharge converts the fluorinated-carbon <electrode> into <carbon> and <lithium> fluoride. It does not simply insert <lithium> into a host that can be reversibly deintercalated. Reversing <electric current> does not cleanly reconstruct the original distributed C–F bonding and <electrode> microstructure.

Insulating LiF, loss of the original reaction interfaces and unwanted <electrolyte> reactions obstruct reversibility. Replating <lithium> can also produce an irregular metallic deposit and internal shorting. A thermodynamically possible formal reverse reaction is therefore not evidence of a practical rechargeable cell.

= iii
{parent=11}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

If the <voltage> were held at $4.57\,\mathrm V$ for the entire ideal discharge, then
$$
\boxed{E=VQ=(4.57)(175.2)=800.664\,\mathrm{Wh}=0.801\,\mathrm{kWh}},
$$
and the electrical <power> would be
$$
\boxed{P=VI=(4.57)(0.010)=0.0457\,\mathrm W=45.7\,\mathrm{mW}}.
$$
These are **constant-voltage estimates**, not a determination of the actual <energy> from the fresh-cell <open-circuit voltage>. Under load the <voltage> depends on state of discharge, <electric polarization> and internal resistance. The physical result is $E=I\int_0^{t_f}V_{\rm load}(t)\,dt$, and $P(t)=IV_{\rm load}(t)$. The supplied single open-circuit value does not specify that <voltage> curve; practical delivered <energy> will generally be lower than the simple estimate.

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Increase the **electrochemically accessible area and reduce transport resistance** rather than merely forcing more <electric current> through the same thick <electrode>. A thinner, porous composite distributed over a larger area, a conductive-carbon network, good <electrolyte> wetting and short ionic paths reduce local <current density> and <electric polarization>. Adequate <battery separator> conductivity and low-resistance collectors also help. Parallel cells can distribute total <electric current> while retaining the total active-material inventory.

The objective is to preserve usable capacity at a higher rate by avoiding premature <voltage> cutoff and inaccessible active material. It does not create more charge: a fixed inventory with $175.2\,\mathrm{Ah}$ delivers a greater <electric current> for a proportionally shorter time. Maintaining the original two-year duration at greater <electric current> requires proportionally more capacity.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=12}
{scope}

= a
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Absorbed <photons> with <energy> at least the <band gap> generate <electron>–hole pairs. The built-in field of the <p-n junction> separates carriers generated in or reaching the <depletion region>; <minority carriers> generated nearby can diffuse into that collection region. Selective contacts carry <electrons> and holes into an external circuit before they recombine.

Define positive <electric current> into the positive-voltage terminal. In the ideal illuminated-diode model,
$$
I(V)=I_0\left[\exp\!\left(\frac{qV}{n k_BT}\right)-1\right]-I_{\rm ph}.
$$
The dark curve has $I_{\rm ph}=0$; illumination shifts it downward. At short circuit $I\simeq-I_{\rm ph}$; at open circuit $I=0$ and $V=V_{\rm oc}$. Between them the illuminated cell lies in **quadrant IV** and supplies <power> $P_{\rm out}=-VI>0$. The <maximum power point> maximizes the area of the rectangle with sides $V$ and $-I$:
$$
\boxed{\frac{d(-VI)}{dV}=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad I+V\frac{dI}{dV}=0}.
$$
It is neither the open-circuit point nor the short-circuit point, both of which supply zero <power>.

\Image[paper-1-solar-iv.png]
{title=Dark and illuminated diode curves and the photovoltaic maximum power point}

= b
{parent=i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At fixed spectrum and <temperature>, photocurrent is approximately proportional to <irradiance>, so the magnitude of <short-circuit current> increases nearly linearly. In the ideal model,
$$
V_{\rm oc}=n\frac{k_BT}{q}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{I_{\rm ph}}{I_0}\right),
$$
so <open-circuit voltage> rises only logarithmically with <irradiance>. More incident optical <power> generally raises the maximum electrical <power>, within the device's thermal and resistive limits.

Increasing <temperature> strongly increases the dark saturation <electric current>. In <silicon> this normally reduces <open-circuit voltage> and maximum <power>, despite a small increase in <short-circuit current> associated with the <temperature> dependence of absorption and <band gap>. The thermal-voltage factor alone is insufficient to predict the sign; the <temperature> dependence of $I_0$ matters.

**<maximum power point tracking> adjusts the load seen by the cell** as <irradiance> and <temperature> change. A controlled DC–DC converter can transform the external load so that operation stays near the changing maximum of $-VI$, instead of at the generally suboptimal intersection with a fixed load. It matches operating conditions and cannot generate additional <energy> beyond the photovoltaic output.

= ii
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The illuminated area and incident <power> are
$$
A=(0.04)^2=1.6\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2},\qquad
P_{\rm in}=87A=0.1392\,\mathrm W.
$$
A $412\,\mathrm{nm}$ <photon> has <energy> $hc/\lambda=4.821\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm J=3.01\,\mathrm{eV}$, above the <silicon> <band gap>. With one collected <electron> per <photon>,
$$
I_{\rm ph}=q\frac{P_{\rm in}\lambda}{hc}=0.0462562\,\mathrm A.
$$
Use the ideal-diode approximation with **ideality factor $n=1$**, negligible shunt leakage and the same dark <diode> parameters under illumination. At $300\,\mathrm K$, $V_T=k_BT/q=0.025852\,\mathrm V$. The measured dark point then fixes
$$
I_0=\frac{10^{-4}}{\exp(0.466/V_T)-1}=1.4844\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm A.
$$
At open circuit the forward <diode> <electric current> balances the photocurrent:
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm oc}=V_T\ln\!\left(1+\frac{I_{\rm ph}}{I_0}\right)=0.62465\,\mathrm V\simeq0.625\,\mathrm V}.
$$
This is below the $1.1\,\mathrm{eV}$ <band gap> expressed per elementary charge. Excess <photon> <energy> does not translate directly into terminal <voltage>; much of it thermalizes.

The paper does not explicitly specify an ideality factor. If it is not taken as unity, one dark operating point cannot determine both $I_0$ and $n$. The corresponding family is
$$
V_{\rm oc}(n)=nV_T\ln\!\left[1+\frac{I_{\rm ph}}{10^{-4}}\left(\exp\!\left(\frac{0.466}{nV_T}\right)-1\right)\right].
$$
Thus $0.625\,\mathrm V$ is a stated-model estimate, not a parameter-free inference from that one measurement.
